{{!--
  The return hop. This page exists for about a second: callback.js verifies the
  state, trades the code for a token against accounts.spotify.com, and replaces
  the URL with the score the listener left from.

  It has no error state on purpose. Every way this can fail — a refused
  consent, the five-user wall, a dead handshake — leaves by the same door, back
  to the score, where the tracklist is waiting. An error page here would be the
  one thing tier 2 exists to prevent.

  The server puts the public client id on this element and reads nothing else.
  The authorization code in the query string is the browser's business.
--}}
<section class="callback" id="callback" data-client-id="{{spotifyClientId}}">
  <p class="callback-eyebrow">spotify</p>
  <h1>Coming back.</h1>
  <p class="callback-note">
    Trading a handshake for permission to write one private playlist. This takes
    a second, and then you&rsquo;re back at the score.
  </p>
  <p class="callback-fallback">
    <noscript>This last step needs JavaScript. <a href="/">Start again from the front page.</a></noscript>
  </p>
</section>
